Subject: [PATCH] libavformat/libsmbclient: avoid double cleanup in libsmbc_close

Calling smbc_close(fd) explicitly before smbc_free_context(ctx, 1)
double-cleans the underlying SMB file: shutdown_ctx=1 already force-closes
every open file inside the context. Stacking two cli_close paths through
Samba's non-reentrant talloc_stackframe machinery is what triggers
"Freed frame ... expected ..." mismatches and the subsequent crash in
cli_close/tevent_req_poll when a read/seek frame is still in flight
(reproducible by seeking into an uncached region of an SMB stream while
paused, then resuming playback).

This patch makes libsmbc_close:
  - delegate fd cleanup to smbc_free_context(ctx, 1) when ctx exists,
  - detach the per-context user data and the thread-local current
    context first to break any dangling back-pointers held by libsmbclient
    callbacks,
  - keep an explicit smbc_close(fd) only on the (rare) ctx==NULL path
    where smbc_free_context cannot help.

--- a/libavformat/libsmbclient.c
+++ b/libavformat/libsmbclient.c
@@ -80,13 +80,23 @@
 static av_cold int libsmbc_close(URLContext *h)
 {
     LIBSMBContext *libsmbc = h->priv_data;
-    if (libsmbc->fd >= 0) {
-        smbc_close(libsmbc->fd);
-        libsmbc->fd = -1;
-    }
+    /* MPVKit patch: do not call smbc_close(fd) explicitly before
+     * smbc_free_context(ctx, 1) — the shutdown_ctx=1 path already
+     * force-closes every open file. Stacking two cli_close paths
+     * through Samba's non-reentrant talloc_stackframe machinery is
+     * what triggers "Freed frame ... expected ..." mismatches when a
+     * read/seek frame is still in flight (e.g. seeking into an
+     * uncached region after a pause). Also detach the per-context
+     * user data and the thread-local current context first. */
     if (libsmbc->ctx) {
+        smbc_setOptionUserData(libsmbc->ctx, NULL);
+        smbc_set_context(NULL);
         smbc_free_context(libsmbc->ctx, 1);
         libsmbc->ctx = NULL;
+        libsmbc->fd  = -1;
+    } else if (libsmbc->fd >= 0) {
+        smbc_close(libsmbc->fd);
+        libsmbc->fd = -1;
     }
     return 0;
 }
